\section{Using commons with other coding agents}{

\code{commons()} is the entry point to use commons with external coding agents.
The approach depends on whether the coding agent already has a persistent
R session. For example, Posit Assistant connects to the user's R session.
Claude Code and Codex, by contrast, do not have a built-in connection to a
persistent R REPL.

\strong{For agents that already have an R session, like Posit Assistant}, create
a \code{commons()} object in the relevant \code{.Rprofile} and then add a line telling
the agent to print it out before doing data analysis:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode markdown">}}\preformatted{The R session has an object called `commons` that holds this team's trusted
code and context. Before analyzing data, run `print(commons)`, and use its
calculations where they apply in favor of writing analysis code ad-hoc.
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Claude Code and Codex have no persistent R session of their own.} They can
get one through \href{https://posit-dev.github.io/mcp-console/}{MCP Console}.
Its R session reads the project's \code{.Rprofile} like any other, so build
\code{commons} there in the same way, and then edit the console tool's
description to tell the model to print out \code{commons} before analyzing data.

To use MCP console, register it for the project, from the project directory:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode bash">}}\preformatted{uvx mcp-console configure-client claude --directory .
uvx mcp-console configure-client codex --directory .
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Then configure MCP Console to offer the model only R, so that it queries
data through \code{commons$run_sql()} rather than sending SQL to a database
itself, and to mention \code{commons} in its tool description. A configured
description replaces the one MCP Console generates, so start from an export
of the resolved configuration:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode bash">}}\preformatted{mkdir -p .agents/console
uvx mcp-console config resolve --full --no-project-config \\
  -c 'languages=[r]' > .agents/console/config.yaml
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

In that file, keep \verb{languages: [r]}, and add the same sentence to the end
of the exported description:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{languages:
  - r
tool:
  description: |-
    # ...the exported description, unchanged...

    The R session has an object called `commons` that holds this team's
    trusted code and context. Before analyzing data, run
    `print(commons)`, and use its calculations where they apply in favor of
    writing analysis code ad-hoc.
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Claude Code shows the tool description only once the agent loads the tool,
so add the sentence to the end of \code{tool.instructions} as well.

\verb{$context()} needs one more step for now. This applies even without a
context layer when a data source has a data dictionary or semantic model,
since \verb{$context()} searches those too. It relies on DuckDB's \code{fts} extension, which MCP Console's sandbox
can't download. MCP Console can install the extension itself, but only
into its own cache, which R's duckdb doesn't read by default. Point R at
that cache with an absolute path (\code{~} and environment variables aren't
expanded):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{environment:
  # macOS; on Linux, /home/you/.cache/mcp-console/dependencies/duckdb
  DUCKDB_R_HOME: /Users/you/Library/Caches/mcp-console/dependencies/duckdb
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Then add one more sentence to both \code{tool.description} and
\code{tool.instructions}, so the agent asks MCP Console to install the
extension:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode markdown">}}\preformatted{The first time you run `commons$context()`, include
`"requirements": \{"duckdb": ["fts"]\}` in the same call.
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

In either setup, \verb{$run_sql()} refuses queries that write, but that check
isn't a security boundary. Code that the agent runs in the same R session
can reach anything the session can, including the connections behind
\code{commons}. Connect to databases with a read-only role.
}

\section{Lifecycle}{

Before commons 0.2.0, \code{commons()} built the agent itself, taking \code{client}
as its first argument. Calls in that form still work, with a deprecation
warning, and return an agent as before. Use \code{\link[=agent]{agent()}} instead.
}

